    }), "\n", _jsx(_components.p, {
      children: "The CAP theorem, formalized by Eric Brewer and proven by Seth Gilbert and Nancy Lynch, states that distributed systems can guarantee at most two of three properties: Consistency (linearizability), Availability (every request receives a response), and Partition tolerance (system continues operating despite network partitions). Since network partitions are inevitable in distributed systems, the real choice is between consistency and availability during partitions. CP systems (like HBase, MongoDB with certain configurations) sacrifice availability to maintain consistency during partitions. AP systems (like Cassandra, DynamoDB) sacrifice consistency to maintain availability. Modern systems often provide tunable consistency, allowing applications to choose per-operation trade-offs."
